Starting with @melon-db/db and @melon-db/db-sqlite is the right call: everything else is just sugar on top of these two.

Below is a focused, implementation-ready PRD/RFC for those two packages only.

@melon-db/db – core engine RFC
1. Scope
@melon-db/db is the core runtime for the “next WatermelonDB”:

Schema + metadata

Internal query AST

Storage adapter interface

Database/collection abstractions

Transactions and subscriptions

It is React-agnostic and storage-agnostic, like WatermelonDB’s Database + DatabaseAdapter + Collection objects, which separate reactive high-level objects from the low-level imperative database world.

2. Goals
Provide a database-agnostic core that can run on SQLite first, but can be adapted to other backends later.

Preserve WatermelonDB-style lazy, local-first, reactive behavior: Database owns an adapter and collections; collections manage records and queries.

Be fully TypeScript-first with strong types for schema, queries, adapters, and runtime.

Keep the API functional and pure where possible; no decorators or heavy inheritance.

3. Non-goals
No React dependency or hooks here.

No Prisma schema parsing, no Mango parsing; only the AST.

No sync protocol or remote concepts; that goes into @melon-db/sync.

4. Functional requirements
4.1 Schema and metadata
Define code-first schema types:

DatabaseSchemaDefinition

CollectionSchemaDefinition

FieldDefinition

RelationDefinition

Provide a MelonSchema runtime object that:

Exposes schema version and collection metadata.

Validates basic invariants (unique collection names, valid relations).

Support versioned schema for future migrations.

This echoes WatermelonDB’s pattern of defining schema and model metadata up front before constructing the Database.

4.2 Query AST
Define a single internal query representation:

QueryAst (collection, where, orderBy, limit, skip, select, mode).

QueryBooleanNode (and/or/not + predicates).

QueryPredicate (field, operator, value).

QueryPlan and PreparedQuery (AST + planning hints).

This AST is the only thing adapters receive for read operations.

WatermelonDB already treats queries as descriptions (“QueryDescription”) which adapters translate into database-specific queries; we’re formalizing that as the AST.

4.3 Storage adapter contract
Define StorageAdapter interface:

Lifecycle:

initialize(schema: MelonSchema)

close()

Query:

prepare(query: PreparedQuery) (optional optimization)

find(query: PreparedQuery) => rows

count(query: PreparedQuery) => count

Writes:

write(operation: AdapterWriteOperation) (insert/update/delete/batch)

transaction(fn)

Change tracking:

getChangedCollections?(sinceVersion)

observeQuery?(query, onChange) => unsubscribe

WatermelonDB explicitly defines adapters as the translation layer between their query language and the database; this interface carries that forward but decouples from any specific syntax.

4.4 Runtime engine
createDatabase({ schema, adapter }) => MelonDatabase:

Holds a MelonSchema + StorageAdapter.

Manages collections and read/write coordination.

MelonDatabase:

collection(name) => MelonCollection.

read(fn) and write(fn) with Watermelon-style guarantees that writers are serialized and readers run without concurrent writers.

unsafeReset() for dev/test usage.

MelonCollection:

findById(id)

findMany(queryAst?)

findFirst(queryAst?)

count(queryAst?)

query(queryAst?) => MelonQueryHandle

insert, update, delete

MelonQueryHandle:

fetch(), fetchOne(), fetchCount()

observe(onValue) => unsubscribe using adapter’s observeQuery if available, otherwise a DB-level subscription.

This aligns with WatermelonDB’s architecture: Database owns an adapter, Collections manage records, and there’s an explicit distinction between read and write actions.

5. Non-functional requirements
Performance: Must preserve Watermelon-style lazy, SQLite-backed queries for large datasets.

Stability: Core types must be stable enough that query layers and sync can build on them.

DX: Clean TS types for schema, collections, adapters.

6. Implementation milestones
M0 – Design & scaffolding (1–2 weeks)
Finalize TS types for schema, AST, adapter, runtime.

Create packages/melon-db with:

src/schema.ts, src/ast.ts, src/adapter.ts, src/database.ts

Initial unit test scaffolding.

Validate design against WatermelonDB’s Architecture + DatabaseAdapters docs.

Exit: Type-safe interfaces compiling, basic createDatabase signature in place.

M1 – In-memory adapter & core behavior (2–3 weeks)
Implement createInMemoryAdapter() inside @melon-db/db (or in testkit if you want to split later).

Implement MelonDatabase and MelonCollection backed by in-memory adapter.

Implement:

read, write with serialized writers.

query that wraps AST and calls adapter.find / count.

Tests:

Simple CRUD + query flows.

Writer invariants (no writes outside write).

Exit: End-to-end CRUD and query with in-memory adapter; no SQLite yet.

M2 – Change notifications & subscriptions (2–3 weeks)
Implement collection-level change tracking and observeQuery fallback (e.g., subscribe to table and recompute).

Ensure the subscription model can map cleanly to React hooks later.

Tests around observe semantics.

Exit: Query handle can be observed and emits value changes consistently.

@melon-db/db-sqlite – SQLite adapter RFC
1. Scope
@melon-db/db-sqlite provides a production StorageAdapter backed by SQLite:

React Native (new architecture, JSI/TurboModule)

Node (where possible) for tooling/tests

It is the main way to realize WatermelonDB’s “lazy, SQLite, separate thread” performance characteristics.

2. Goals
Efficient translation of PreparedQuery → SQL statements.

Support transactions and batched writes.

Support reactive queries where platform allows (e.g., triggers + change notifications).

Be identical in behavior across RN and Node where feasible.

3. Non-goals
No query building/parsing (AST is input).

No schema migration planning (schema is in @melon-db/db).

No sync; change tracking hooks are an optional interface for sync to consume later.

4. Functional requirements
4.1 SQL compilation
Implement a compiler from QueryAst to parameterized SQL:

mode: 'many' → SELECT ...

mode: 'one' → SELECT ... LIMIT 1

mode: 'count' → SELECT COUNT(*) ...

Map predicates to SQL operators:

eq, neq, gt, gte, lt, lte, in, notIn, like, isNull.

Support orderBy, limit, skip with safe bounds.

Log/debug SQL (in dev) for devtools integration.

WatermelonDB currently depends on SQLite as the main engine and emphasizes lazy loading via SQLite queries, so we preserve that pattern.

4.2 Adapter implementation
Implement StorageAdapter for SQLite:

initialize(schema) – create tables/indexes if needed.

find, count – execute compiled SQL via SQLite binding.

write – map insert/update/delete operations to SQL statements, using transactions where appropriate.

transaction – wrap in SQLite transaction.

Optionally support getChangedCollections for sync usage later (e.g., tracking last_modified rows as in Watermelon sync docs).

4.3 RN new architecture support
Provide a JSI/TurboModule-backed implementation for RN.

Document RN version constraints and any limitations.

Validate with a small RN app using new arch.

4.4 Node support (optional but desirable)
Implement a Node-only build using a Node SQLite binding.

This can be used for migration tooling, test harnesses, and server-side scripts.

5. Non-functional requirements
Performance:

Comparable to WatermelonDB for large local datasets (10k+ rows).

Stability:

Strong guarantees around transactional writes.

DX:

Clear error messages with SQL and AST context in dev.

6. Implementation milestones
M0 – SQL generator design (1–2 weeks)
Design SQL target for the AST including:

How boolean and/or/not map to WHERE clauses.

Handling include / joins (can be v1-limited).

Write a pure TS module that turns QueryAst into { sql, params }.

Unit tests over AST → SQL mapping.

Exit: SQL generator tested independently of SQLite.

M1 – Node SQLite adapter (2–3 weeks)
Integrate a Node SQLite binding.

Implement StorageAdapter methods for Node.

Implement initialize(schema) to create tables/indexes from DatabaseSchemaDefinition.

Implement find, count, write, transaction.

Exit: In apps/playground-node or tests, you can use @melon-db/db + @melon-db/db-sqlite for CRUD and queries.

M2 – RN new architecture adapter (3–4 weeks)
Wrap SQLite in a JSI/TurboModule module for RN.

Implement the same StorageAdapter interface using native calls.

Validate with a minimal RN example app (no sync, just CRUD).

Document setup steps.

Exit: RN example app using MelonDatabase with SQLite adapter works on device.

M3 – Observability & perf (2–3 weeks)
Add timing info, basic profiling hooks (for devtools).

Benchmark:

Insert 10k / 50k / 100k records.

Query latency for typical flows.

Compare with a baseline (WatermelonDB where possible).

Coordination between the two
To keep you moving efficiently:

Do @melon-db/db M0/M1 first using the in-memory adapter.

Then implement @melon-db/db-sqlite M0/M1:

SQL generator and Node adapter can use the same tests as in-memory, just with different adapters.

Once both are stable:

Wire RN new-arch integration and the first playground app.

Only after that, start @melon-db/db-query and @melon-db/db-react.